# Harr host adapter: Codex exposes the LeanCTX tool names directly.
CTX_READ=ctx_read
CTX_SHELL=ctx_shell
CTX_SEARCH=ctx_search
CTX_GLOB=ctx_glob
CTX_TOOLS=ctx_tools
CTX_CALL=ctx_call
HOST_ORCHESTRATION_POLICY='- Codex orchestration economy: keep sequential implementation in the root regardless of task size; do not spawn a native worker merely to move implementation context out of the root. Spawn native workers only for genuinely independent parallel scopes. The root owns decomposition and integration; give each worker a bounded, self-sufficient scope and avoid dependencies between active workers. While a worker is active, it owns that delegated scope; the root and sibling workers must not duplicate it. Workers never spawn/fork agents or invoke Asa themselves; the root is the sole agent router. If a worker reaches an unresolved material architecture decision, pause that scope and return compact evidence instead of choosing locally. On that escalation, the root must not reread or re-analyze the delegated scope: it may add only already-known global/user constraints, route the compact handoff through `$asa`, then forward the decision/invariants back to the SAME worker. For an unresolved material architecture decision discovered directly by root, root gathers missing repository/runtime/contract facts under normal Harr Workspace/tool-routing policy before invoking `$asa`, but once the boundary is material root must not invent, rank, compare, or choose architecture alternatives. Asa uses full inherited history, so do not retransmit a duplicate full evidence packet; include concise verified anchors for decisive facts that exist only in tool output or may be non-durable after compaction. If the user explicitly asks Asa herself to analyze selected code, an image, or other material, root (or the delegated scope owner) still prepares the relevant evidence/constraints and locates the exact material, but does not perform that requested deep analysis first; Asa may analyze the supplied material without broadening into repository discovery or gathering missing surrounding evidence. If explicit analysis discovers a new material architecture decision the user did not ask Asa to resolve, Asa returns the exact escalation question and root asks the user before continuing the SAME Asa via followup_task. After Asa returns the requested decision or analysis, the existing implementation owner/context continues.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY='Prefer LeanCTX for workspace investigation. Native file read/search/glob may be used only when the target is outside LeanCTX addressable paths or LeanCTX is unavailable; keep those fallbacks narrow and bounded. Native shell is not a search/read fallback for workspace files: do not emulate ctx_search/ctx_glob/ctx_read with rg/grep/find/sed/awk/cat/head/tail. Use native shell only for genuine CLI operations when the Harr routing above permits them, except for the Codex memory lookup below.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY+=' Codex memory exception (only for $CODEX_HOME/memories/ or ~/.codex/memories/): This is Codex-owned host memory outside the Harr workspace and LeanCTX allowed roots. A memory quick pass is not repository investigation: do not use CodeGraph-first, ctx_search, ctx_read, ctx_glob, or ctx_shell to read Codex memory. Follow the Codex memory instructions already in the prompt; memory_summary.md is already supplied there and must not be reopened.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY+=' When further memory evidence is necessary, use the host-native exec_command for a bounded literal or keyword search with rg -n -m 20 against the specific MEMORY.md file, then read only the identified line range with sed -n START,ENDp. Follow links to at most the relevant rollout summaries as specified by Codex memory instructions. Use read-only, single-purpose commands, keep stdout bounded, and do not repeat a successful read. Never use python -c, python3 -c, Path.read_text, or a shell script/pipeline as an alternative way to read/search Codex memory.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY+=' This exception applies to Codex memory only; all workspace code/config/doc search and reads still follow Harr LeanCTX and CodeGraph routing, and it does not grant access to other paths under ~/.codex/ or change LeanCTX PathJail.'
HOST_NATIVE_POLICY+=' Codex Desktop Ein app routing: when a configured named Ein direct MCP App tool <name>::ein is available, use that direct tool for user-authorized start and resume so its inline completion marker is attached to the exact job. Use the LeanCTX gateway for status, messages, wait, cancel, and as the fallback when the direct Codex app tool is unavailable. This exception applies only to Codex Desktop; OpenCode and other hosts keep Harr-managed MCP calls behind LeanCTX.'
